<ProseLayout
  title="The business skills were never part of the developer curriculum."
  description="Why Developer Business Lab builds practical business systems for technically capable independent developers."
  eyebrow="About Developer Business Lab"
>
  <p>You can learn to build a production application without ever learning how to decide what service to sell, set a sustainable price, create a client pipeline, run a sales conversation, control a proposal, or turn a successful project into repeat work.</p>

  <p>That gap becomes obvious the moment you work for yourself. The technical work may be the part you are best at, while the business around it is assembled from Reddit threads, YouTube videos, books, marketplace habits, advice from other freelancers, and whatever you had to figure out during the last project.</p>

  <h2>Why the Lab exists</h2>

  <p>Each product takes one commercial problem and turns it into a system you can run. The goal is not to make you sound like a marketer. It is to help you make better business decisions around work you already know how to do.</p>

  <p>That means clear boundaries. A pricing guide should tell you what it does not price. A proposal kit should not pretend to replace legal counsel. A prospecting system should be allowed to reject a lead when the evidence is weak. A SaaS validation system should be allowed to tell you not to build.</p>

  <h2>Evidence first</h2>

  <p>Developer Business Lab does not invent testimonials, customer results, revenue figures, technical defects, or certainty that the evidence cannot support. Examples should be concrete enough to inspect, and claims should be traceable to reasoning, demonstrations, credible sources, or clearly identified fictional examples.</p>

  <h2>Who this is for</h2>

  <p>If you can deliver the technical work but still feel like the offer, price, pipeline, sales process, or next stage of the business gets rebuilt from scratch, start with that bottleneck.</p>

  <p><Button as="a" href="/start/">Find the System You Need</Button></p>
</ProseLayout>
